## First design profile published for review

Draft public implementation profile: [PR #6736](https://github.com/Luminous-Dynamics/symtropy/pull/6736).

It adds:
- household H0 / neighbourhood N1 / regional R2 / qualified industrial Q3 capability tiers;
- separate P0–P3 part-risk classes;
- domestic printer-emissions and chemical-control boundary;
- rights-cleared design-to-accepted-part data package requirements;
- full cost per accepted part and matched restoration-time benchmark;
- fail-closed examples: packaging spacer and assembly jig remain not admitted; a fan guard is blocked from H0/N1.

Static validation performed on the exact PR branch: fetched both changed files, parsed the JSON example file, verified the tier/part-class separation and seeded fan-guard exclusion. PR head is `7843ded640c76cccea8450b840cfb9258546dab3`; no workflow run was returned for that commit. This is a draft documentation increment, not a qualified manufacturing process or part catalogue.

### Household-vs-hub economics — use measured demand, not printer ownership as the metric

A November 2026 South African retail price guide lists capable consumer FDM machines around R8,000–R11,000 and filament examples near R200–R400/kg. These are dynamic guide prices, not quotations:
- https://www.jlscomputers.co.za/3d-printing/3d-printer-price-south-africa/
- https://createit3d.co.za/blogs/news/how-much-does-a-3d-printer-cost-in-south-africa

Illustrative capex-only sensitivity for an R8,000–R11,000 printer amortized over five years:
- 10 accepted parts/year: R160–R220 machine capital per accepted part;
- 100 accepted parts/year: R16–R22;
- 250 accepted parts/year: R6.40–R8.80.

These exclude material, failed prints, electricity, operator time, maintenance, enclosure/ventilation, inspection and transport. The result does not prove a hub is cheaper, but shows why occasional appliance-spare demand alone is unlikely to justify a printer in every home. Household ownership may still make sense when it serves wider education, personal fabrication and household-accessory demand; this has to be measured in a matched utilization model.

Next evidence gate is not procurement: freeze one P0 artifact, its rights/material/process envelope, measure a real OEM/conventional route and compare household vs shared-hub access with full denominators.
